  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 344 PM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nAreawide, temperatures have climbed into the low/mid 90s amidst \ndaytime heating and ample warm/moist transport from the southwest on \nthe back edge of a low-level ridge. Presently, a weak band of \nshowers/storms have begun to pop up along the slowly advancing cold \nfront situated right at the intersection of the IA/NE/MO state \nlines. This cold front, along with a slowly encroaching \nshortwave situated over central Kansas, will be the main story \nfor the short term forecast period.\n\nAs the cold front continues slowly pushing southeastward, it will \nencounter an airmass with ample instability (2000-2500 J/kg MUCAPE) \nand increasing shear (30-35 kts). With ascent along the cold front \nexacerbated by the oncoming shortwave and its associated vorticity \nadvection/height falls, the potential for thunderstorm development \nacross eastern Kansas and western Missouri this evening continues to \nlook likely (60-75%). Looking to specific hazards with these \nthunderstorms, the signal continues to trend towards severe \nwind/hail, with steepening mid-level lapse rates out ahead of the \ncold front and elevated DCAPE ~1000 J/kg. However, the greater \nopportunity for larger hail resides further towards the southwest of \nthe forecast area, as storms initiate along the boundary amidst \nhigher CAPE and effective bulk shear. The larger focus for severe \nthreats going into the evening will be towards severe wind as storms \ncongeal and become more elevated/cold pool dominant as they push \neast/northeast. There continues to be an additional risk for heavy \nrainfall creating localized hydro concerns, but overnight activity \nlooks to remain progressive enough that even 6 hourly accumulations \nlook to remain below flash flood guidance across the area. However, \nthe environment still remains favorable for efficient rainfall in \nthe strongest storms (PWATs ~2\"), and any training thunderstorms \ncould increase hydro concerns with continued rainfall accumulations, \nespecially if the cold front slows/stalls over time.\n\nSpeaking of the front potentially slowing/stalling... Recent model \nguidance has trended towards slowing down the forward progression of \nthe cold front overnight into tomorrow morning, with storms \nlingering through at least the late morning. Severe potential \ndecreases through the overnight period, but stronger storms could \nstill pose a wind/hail risk with elevated CAPE and continued \nsteepening lapse rates with the front. Potential activity tomorrow \nafternoon/evening will depend on the location of the cold frontal \nboundary in relation to further shortwave perturbations in the \natmospheric flow, as there looks to be some lingering instability in \nthe atmosphere that could be invigorated with synoptic ascent and \norganized by sufficient shear. Recent CAM guidance has not been \nincredibly helpful in agreeing on one particular solution over the \nother, with scenarios ranging from quiet weather to a complex of \nthunderstorms pushing through the forecast area by the late \nafternoon/early evening depending on the placement of the synoptic-\nscale features. With continued uncertainty in the exact position of \nthese mechanisms, have elected to once again lower and broaden PoPs \nacross the area tomorrow afternoon/evening, with the potential \nexisting for scattered showers/storms but uncertainty remaining \nhigher in exact timing/location.\n\nAny remnant activity from Sunday will gradually phase out by Monday \nevening as the cold front continues to push towards the southeast \nout of the forecast area. At the synoptic scale, predominantly \nnortherly flow on the backside of a retreating upper trough will \nintroduce much cooler and drier conditions through the early half of \nnext week, with daytime highs and overnight lows cooling down to the \n60s and 50s, respectively. By the later half of next week, ridging \nlooks to build back across the southern half of the CONUS, with \nguidance progressively indicating warmer and wetter conditions with \nthe intrusion of shortwave perturbations in the upper-level flow off \nthe Rockies.",
